<section style="padding:72px 24px 40px;"><div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;"><div class="rich"><p>A radon test before buying a house sounds simple until you look at the calendar. Health Canada's guideline is 200 Bq/m3 (becquerels per cubic metre, a measure of radioactivity in the air), and it applies to the average level over a year. The shortest test Health Canada accepts for comparing a home against it runs 91 days. If your closing is 30 days out, that test does not fit.</p><p>So the realistic plan has two parts. During the sale, a short-term screening test or a continuous digital monitor gives you a rough read, and C-NRPP (the Canadian National Radon Proficiency Program) says a short-term screening "could be conducted to provide some insight". After possession you run a <a href="/radon-testing/">long-term test</a> and base any decision on that number. Call (204) 555-0173 and we'll put you in touch with an independent C-NRPP certified measurement professional who works in Winnipeg, Regina, Saskatoon or the towns around them.</p></div></div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px 64px;"><div style="max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">What We Handle</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 28px;">What's Included in Every Visit</h2>
    <div class="feat-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:20px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Short-term screening during the condition period</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">A certified measurement professional can run a short-term screen while the offer is still conditional. It tells you roughly where the house sits, but Health Canada says short-term results are not the basis for a mitigation decision.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Continuous digital monitors</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Electronic monitors log readings across the test instead of giving one number at the end. During a sale they are screening tools; if you later use one for a long-term test, Health Canada says pick one on C-NRPP's approved list.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Placement that holds up</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">The detector goes in the lowest lived-in level, in the breathing zone at least 30 cm above the floor and 30 cm from walls. Keep it away from sumps, vents, windows and heat sources, or the result means very little.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Long-term test after possession</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">At least 91 days, with 91 of them in the heating season; 3 to 12 months is better. HomeRadonTest.ca sells a kit for $70.00 before tax, with shipping both ways and the lab analysis included.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Seller-side testing done early</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">A seller who starts a long-term test months before listing has a real annual-average number to hand a buyer. That usually beats a rushed screen run in the middle of the deal.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Checking a house that already has a system</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">If there's a pipe and fan in the basement, the fan should be running and the U-tube manometer should show a level difference between its two sides. Ask the seller for the test result taken after the system went in.</p>
      </div>
</div>
